WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.984 --> 00:00:01.704
Welcome back.

00:00:02.254 --> 00:00:06.274
In module one, we covered who
GuestGuard is, why this work matters,

00:00:06.634 --> 00:00:09.234
and what the inspection process
looks like from a high level.

00:00:10.174 --> 00:00:12.324
This module is where we
get into the details.

00:00:12.924 --> 00:00:16.694
We're going to walk through the full
GuestGuard inspection rubric room by room,

00:00:16.954 --> 00:00:22.064
category by category, so that by the time
you step into your first property, nothing

00:00:22.064 --> 00:00:23.734
on that question list surprises you.

00:00:24.743 --> 00:00:29.004
The rubric covers eight areas of the
property: the exterior, the basement

00:00:29.004 --> 00:00:34.074
or maintenance room, the kitchen, the
bathroom, the hallway, the bedroom,

00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:38.544
the common room, and an overall section
that wraps up the property as a whole.

00:00:39.893 --> 00:00:43.193
Within each area, you'll find questions
grouped into focus categories,

00:00:43.474 --> 00:00:48.054
things like safety and security,
cleanliness, accessibility, and so on.

00:00:48.603 --> 00:00:50.803
We'll move through these
categories in order in each room.

00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:52.453
Let's start outside.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:56.213
Your inspection starts before
you walk through the front door.

00:00:56.683 --> 00:00:59.533
The exterior of the property
tells you a lot about how a host

00:00:59.544 --> 00:01:02.873
manages their property overall,
and it covers some important

00:01:03.173 --> 00:01:07.843
safety and access considerations,
such as emergency evacuation.

00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:10.413
Right away, you'll be
asked about walkways.

00:01:10.673 --> 00:01:14.013
Are they clear of obstructions, and
are they marked with legible signage?

00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:16.393
This matters for emergency egress.

00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:21.004
If something goes wrong and guests need to
move fast, they need an unobstructed path.

00:01:21.554 --> 00:01:25.473
If signage is missing, your job is to
flag it so the host can install it.

00:01:26.663 --> 00:01:29.473
Regarding accessibility, next is parking.

00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:31.004
Two questions here.

00:01:31.463 --> 00:01:34.924
Is parking within thirty feet of the
front door, and is there at least

00:01:34.924 --> 00:01:38.423
twenty-four inches of clearance
on each side of a parked car?

00:01:38.853 --> 00:01:41.263
These are specifically
designed for wheelchair users.

00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:45.594
Close parking and room to open a car
door fully make a material difference

00:01:45.603 --> 00:01:47.294
for guests with mobility needs.

00:01:48.673 --> 00:01:52.483
You'll also document whether the property
has a lockbox or digital-coded lock,

00:01:52.993 --> 00:01:55.003
and if so, take a photo of it for us.

00:01:55.623 --> 00:01:58.694
You'll also confirm that off-street
parking is available and well-lit.

00:01:59.303 --> 00:02:02.124
These are basic logistics, but
they affect the guest's first

00:02:02.124 --> 00:02:03.653
impression of the property.

00:02:05.368 --> 00:02:10.018
Within safety and security, here you're
checking four things: stair handrails on

00:02:10.018 --> 00:02:15.448
any exterior steps, potential trip hazards
on walkways like an unexpected change

00:02:15.448 --> 00:02:20.318
in floor height or an uneven pavement,
clear paths at the time of inspection,

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.318
and whether shrubbery is trimmed.

00:02:22.977 --> 00:02:24.978
That last one isn't just about aesthetics.

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:29.738
Overgrown bushes near the property
provide a cover for intruders.

00:02:30.157 --> 00:02:31.398
Flag it if it's an issue.

00:02:32.258 --> 00:02:35.447
Regarding outdoor features, this
is where the conditional questions

00:02:35.457 --> 00:02:36.827
start to show up in a big way.

00:02:37.428 --> 00:02:39.668
The first question is
simple: Is there a pool?

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:43.087
If yes, the whole subsection
for pools opens up.

00:02:44.248 --> 00:02:47.898
For pools, you'll check that rules
are posted, chemicals are locked

00:02:47.898 --> 00:02:52.617
away, the pool meets general
visual inspection standards, and

00:02:52.617 --> 00:02:54.288
that adequate fencing is in place.

00:02:55.058 --> 00:02:57.797
If there's fencing, you'll
measure the height and largest

00:02:57.808 --> 00:02:59.507
vertical and horizontal gaps.

00:02:59.908 --> 00:03:02.588
You'll also confirm whether there
is a gate and whether that gate

00:03:02.588 --> 00:03:04.648
locks, even if a latch counts.

00:03:06.268 --> 00:03:10.507
Hot tubs follow the same patterns:
rules posted, chemicals stored

00:03:10.508 --> 00:03:15.378
properly, general condition, and
whether the cover is on when not in use.

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:18.947
That last point is specifically
about child and pet safety.

00:03:19.877 --> 00:03:23.268
You'll also note whether there are
outdoor cooking equipment and a patio

00:03:23.268 --> 00:03:27.327
area, and whether both of those meet
general visual standards if those

00:03:27.337 --> 00:03:28.987
sections are available to guests.

00:03:30.498 --> 00:03:33.318
Second to last within this section
is utilities and cleanliness.

00:03:34.087 --> 00:03:39.387
Two quick items here: GFCI outlets in
any wet outdoor areas and an overall

00:03:39.398 --> 00:03:41.208
visual assessment of the exterior.

00:03:41.987 --> 00:03:46.018
Is the property clean, well-maintained,
and free of obvious hazards?

00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:48.238
Here you're going to trust
your professional eye.

00:03:49.947 --> 00:03:54.227
Lastly, if the host allows pets,
which you'll confirm in the overall

00:03:54.227 --> 00:03:58.627
section before starting the inspection,
three exterior questions apply.

00:03:59.207 --> 00:04:01.717
First, easy walking access nearby.

00:04:02.377 --> 00:04:05.047
Secondly, a fenced space for dogs.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:09.187
And third, pet waste disposal
facilities on the property.


